  "slug": "assembly-read-1791",
  "summary": "Recent preprints report reconstruction tools that control errors at source, from codon alignment to nanopore assembly and RNA search.",
  "synthesis": "Across these preprints, the common thread is that reconstruction and search steps are being redesigned to limit error propagation rather than correct errors downstream. WALRUS in HyPhy 3 estimates codon alignment, phylogeny and selection tests jointly, and is reported to reduce indel-driven false positives in simulated and avian data. HARICOT assembles raw nanopore signals without basecalling, using fuzzy de Bruijn graphs and Hough-transform overlap detection, and is reported to improve contiguity over comparators. TERRACE 2.0 combines outward paired-end and chimeric reads to assemble circular RNAs from short-read RNA-seq data. Infernal-GPU parallelizes covariance-model scoring on GPUs, recalibrates Rfam E-values and recovers remote structured RNA homologs missed by sequence filters. Two benchmarks examine upstream choices directly: local assembly consensus strategies depend on read type and error profile, and DNA polymerase selection affects HiFi assembly contiguity and completeness. What is new is the explicit evaluation of upstream decisions alongside method-level redesign.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-23"
}
